the long-awaited wastelanders update  comes with a number of changes  improvements and new content with that  in mind you might be asking yourself  should I give fallout 76 another shot  fortunately I've put in 30 hours to help  you answer that very question but before  we dive in I went to answer some burning  questions you probably have about  fallout 76 and its current state first  up this fallout 76 feel like a fallout  game now I will dig more into this later  in the video but the short answer is  kind of the branching quest dialogue  choices and factions bring back warm and  fuzzy memories of Fallout 3 and new  Vegas but the multiplayer seems even  more out of place than it did before if  you've already played a decent amount of  76 you might be wondering if you should  start a new character  honestly this one's kind of up to you  the meat of wastelanders story requires  you to be level 20 but if you're like me  and only played a few hours back when  the game came out in November 2018 it's  probably worth starting fresh and of  course is fallout 76 still buggy yes it  is  it's in a better state than it was at  launch but in the 30 hours I've played  I've personally experienced numerous  crashes broken AI and unresponsive  enemies to name a few when we add in the  crashes and bugs my squad ran into it's  a pretty lengthy list I think I just  crashed I think I just hate to be  surprised because you're up you're  floating oh yeah finally how are those  microtransactions  they are still pretty blatant now I wish  I could say that the microtransactions  are only for cosmetics but that's not  entirely true weapon repair kits which I  found very useful can only be acquired  from the atomic shop you can find  improved weapon repair kits in the world  but after 30 hours I've only found one  now with that out of the way let's talk  about the opening hours of fallout 76  wastelanders  as I mentioned before I decided to start  a new character for a couple reasons  first I wanted to see if the  wastelanders update treated new players  differently second the last time I  played fallout 76 was also the first  time and I don't remember a single thing  about it or the character I created once  you've finished the tutorial and exit  vault 76 you'll meet your first pair of  NPCs whoa whoa just a friend here not a  threat these characters will hint at the  overall narrative but more importantly  you'll have the chance to experiment  with new dialogue mechanics the first  thing you'll notice is that the dialogue  system is more in line with fallout 3  and new Vegas instead of the limiting  conversation wheel from four there's a  familiar text box at the bottom with  multiple responses and more importantly  skill checks this means that not only do  your special points contribute to the  number of perk cards you can equip but  they also play a role in conversations  if you invest heavily in charisma then  you can smooth talk your way through  certain discussions conversely if you  decide to invest heavily in strength you  can intimidate NPCs in departing with  their valuables or relinquishing  important information the first story  arc which involves helping out a nearby  tavern called the wayfarer offers a good  introduction to the story and dialogue  mechanics  after getting to know Dutchess and a few  of her patrons she'll ask you to deal  with some bandits that have been giving  her a hard time in true fallout fashion  you can decide how you want to deal with  them you can do a bit of research by  chatting up the locals you can  sweet-talk your way in and convince them  to leave or you can gun them all down  like yours truly some dialogue even  hints at the possibility of joining up  with these bandits I didn't try this out  personally but I'd be curious to see  where that takes the player now I will  say a lot of these early quests are a  bit straightforward when you compare  them to the likes of Fallout through  your new Vegas but it's comforting to  see and interact with all these NPCs the  world feels more alive and it's way  easier to get invested in the story this  time however before I say too much there  is one huge caveat though I think it's  safe to say that a lot of people either  fell off or never picked up fallout 76  due to its online multiplayer  I was definitely one of those people  while the idea of exploring Appalachia  with a friend or two sounded novel the  execution was rough a  fortunately with wastelanders  multi-player feels even more out of  place talking to NPCs reading quest  information and making important  decisions is a lot tougher when your  teammates are talking in your ear it is  but no it is it's it's what's it called  the green green something whenever I  joined up with friends I tended the tuna  NPC dialogue and skip through important  conversations whenever they started to  drag on it's kind of a shame too because  I was genuinely interested in some of  the characters and what they had to say  now even if you don't care about the  characters in dialogue it's still very  difficult to play weigh sling their  story with a team because you can't  complete story missions and another team  members instance this means that if you  want to progress through the story at  pace with a friend you'll have to enter  mission areas like caves and buildings  on your own you can still join your team  leader if you'd like but you won't be  able to talk to key characters and  collect important objects needed to  advance the story with all that in mind  I recommend playing wastes lenders by  yourself and saving the side quests and  events for when you're with friends and  if you plan on starting a new character  you'll want to do a lot of events in  side quests because after you finish the  wayward story arc you'll need to at  level 20 to progress any further I have  an important mission but you're just not  ready it's too dangerous  I can't in good conscience send you on  it at this point in my adventure I  decided to retrace my steps and continue  fall at 76 is original story which tasks  you with following overseer logs this is  where fall at 76 started to feel more  like well fall at 76 I had to grind out  12 more levels in order to continue and  without NPC based quests I found myself  listening to a lot of audio logs  clearing out camps and partaking in  events this meant a lot of combat and  when all you're doing in fallout 76 is  combat it can be a tough game to enjoy  the aiming is still stiff the menus are  just as cumbersome as before melee  weapons feel floaty and there are still  plenty of bugs  it also doesn't help that most of the  enemies just rush you so I feel like my  go-to strategy is to just backpedal and  shoot that's is a good alternative to  aiming but you can't rely on it all the  time since it's tied to your action  points now I know I said multiplayer  doesn't quite fit in with wastelanders I  would recommend playing with friends  when you're grinding out these levels I  honestly don't think I would have made  it if I was playing by myself the entire  time I will say though to fallouts  credit I did find the world a lot of fun  to explore there are interesting  landmarks to visit colorful areas to  explore and plenty of Secrets to find  despite all these bugs and issues I ran  into I didn't feel like I was wasting my  time when you finally hit level 20  wastelanders starts to open up more the  faction system is introduced along with  2 factions the settlers and the bandits  which admittedly sound way more generic  than what I've come to expect from  fallout but we'll look past that for now  you can also visit their respective  settlements here you can rest it ends  talk to NPCs do some shopping and pick  up quests while these settlements don't  have as much character as something like  Megaton or Novac it's still great to  finally see towns in this game just like  previous Fallout games you will have  various opportunities to aid or ignore  one of the two factions and that will  affect your overall standing major story  beats are tied with which faction you  side with and the leaders you befriend  I've only completed a few quests for the  factions so far but as of now the  bandits seem way more interesting their  leader meg is an irreverent  tough-as-nails bandit who dunks on you  whenever she can I know enough about  people to know they've all got an agenda  but for most that's looking out for  number one  meanwhile the leader of the settlers  page is just kind of boring I'm curious  to see how their stories develop as I  play but right now the settlers feel  kind of one-dimensional and if you want  to kick us out then the answer is no if  the walls weren't already kind of a  statement to that effect now I will say  the faction system as a whole feels a  little shallow but I don't necessarily  think that's a bad thing like I said  before if you plan on playing this game  with a few friends it can be tough to  follow the game's story and carrot  throwing another faction into the mix  might complicate things even more and if  you plan on playing solo like me there's  still enough there to chew on so with  all that out of the way does fallout 76  feel like a fallout game now well I  guess it depends on what a fallout game  is to you if you're in it for the story  characters and tough decisions then yeah  all that stuff is there are the story  and characters as interesting as in  previous games wastelanders certainly  has its moments but if you're expecting  the depth and complexity of fallout  through your new vegas then I would  lower your expectations while  wastelanders may not be a game changer  it certainly breathes life into a game  that needed it if you can look past the  bugs the rough around the edges gameplay  and the blatant monetization then yes  there is a lot of fun to be had in  Appalachia anyway let me know what you  think have you been playing fall at 76  are you enjoying it let me know in the  comments below and keep an eye out for  our updated review on GameSpot calm  [Music]

